module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// A CONFINEMENT WITNESS WHOSE ONLY WRITABLE ROOT IS THE LIVE HOST'S PRODUCTION ROOT IS EFFECTIVELY NEVER
// EXECUTED, AND MUTATES THE LIVE ROOT ON THE ONE HOST WHERE IT RUNS. Found 2026-11-01 on srv2, while
// bringing up the wet corpus there: nine install claims in
// `test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness` failed with
// `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied`, and the spawn claims in
// `test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness` carried the same target. The cause is not
// the host and not the mode arithmetic: both witnesses built the instance under test with
// `roadmap_dashboard_instance.srv1_lab_dashboard_instance`, so `provision_parent_root` came from
// `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent` = `/opt/gunbc-lab`, whose child `srv1_dashboard_lab_root` is the lab's
// instance root — the LIVE srv1 lab tree — and the claims' writes are aimed under that path. srv2 has user `briansrls` but no group `node` and no
// `gunbc-worker`/`gunbc-attempt`, so it cannot write there; srv1 can, and srv1 is the machine the lab serves.
//
// THE INVALID STATE, STATED AS A STATE AND NOT AS AN INCIDENT. A wet witness that (a) derives its install
// target from a production host layout, and (b) asserts a confinement boundary by writing under that target,
// has no host on which its evidence is both obtainable and harmless. On every other host it refuses at the
// first `install`/`mkdir`, so the guarantee its claims name is never executed — the corpus reads the file as
// enrolled (`gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` exclusion row + `v2.workflow.local_repo_wet_terminal` schedule row per
// function) and reports nothing, because nothing ran to fail. On the live host it passes, and the price of
// that PASS is writes into the root a production service serves from. There is no third case.
//
// THE HARM IS BOTH DIRECTIONS AT ONCE, AND ONLY THE SECOND ONE GETS ATTENTION. The direction people notice
// is a witness writing to a live root: the lab's `source`, `bin/gunbc`, `origin.git`, `attempts` and
// `attempt-state` trees are the lab's serving state, and a witness that installs into them has changed what
// the lab dispatches against — with no seal, no rollback and no receipt, because a witness's scratch tree is
// not a store it is allowed to own. The direction people do NOT notice is the one that actually applied here
// for the whole life of these files: the claims never ran, so a confinement guarantee that reads as tested (a
// registered wet claim, in the corpus, with a PASS-shaped name) had no execution behind it at all. DESIGN.md
// section 5 names this exact shape "a claim that cannot fail is a claim that was never executed".
//
// THE DISTINGUISHING FACTS — WHAT SEPARATES THIS STATE FROM AN ORDINARY RED WET CLAIM, AND WHY NOTHING IN THE
// PRESENT CORPUS SEPARATES THEM.
//
//   1. THE REFUSAL IS AT THE TARGET, NOT AT THE GUARANTEE. srv2's nine failures read
//      `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied` — a refusal about where the
//      witness is allowed to exist, not about whether the sandbox confines. A red whose message names a
//      production path is not a red about the guarantee; and the same red, on the host that owns the path, is
//      a mutation.
//   2. THE TARGET IS NOT A LITERAL IN THE TEST, IT IS A PRODUCTION DERIVATION REACHED THROUGH A PRODUCTION
//      FUNCTION. `/opt/gunbc-lab` appears nowhere in either witness file; it arrives as the projection of
//      `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` → `isolated_dashboard_instance_admitting` →
//      `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent`, and the field that carries it (`provision_parent_root`) is set
//      from a sibling field (`lab.provision_parent_root`) that a reader has no reason to suspect. A grep for
//      `/opt` under `dag/test` finds nothing, which is exactly how this survived review.
//   3. THE PRINCIPAL ARRIVES THE SAME WAY. `directory_owner` and `worker: WorkerDedicatedAccount` project the
//      srv1 account facts (uid 1000, group `node`, gid 1003 as measured there), so the witness also asserts
//      ownership against an account database that only srv1 has. An absent group makes `find -chgrp` exit 1
//      (`extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils` says so of find 4.10.0), which is a second host-shaped refusal with the
//      same signature as an ordinary bug.
//   4. THE RUNG CHECK CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE THE RUNG CHECK ASKS THE WRONG QUESTION. The corpus knows this
//      file is registered and wet, and the schedule knows each function is named. Neither asks whether the
//      host running it can reach its target, nor whether the target is one the production model also hands to
//      a privileged writer — which is the same question the production layout in `gunbc.host.host_layout` asks
//      of a PRODUCTION write (is this a root the account owns), asked of a test instead.
//   5. THE FAILURE IS SILENT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ON THE SAME FILE. The strongest version of this defect is the
//      one that never produced the srv2 reds at all: a witness whose target happens to be writable on the
//      runner passes, and its PASS is indistinguishable from the PASS it would print after mutating a live
//      root.
//
// WHAT WOULD REFUSE IT, AND WHAT WOULD NOT. A test of the derivation, or one more assertion inside the same
// witness, cannot: both are written by the party that chose the target, which is DESIGN.md section 3's point
// that a witness and its subject need separate sources of truth. What refuses is a check that reads the
// witness's declared install target from OUTSIDE it and asks two questions no participant in the witness
// answers: is the target host-supplied, and is it a path the production layout hands to a privileged writer.
//
// THE PAIRING OBLIGATION, WHICH IS WHY THIS ROW IS NOT "DELETE THE PRODUCTION DERIVATION". Dropping
// `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` from these witnesses would remove the last execution of the real path, so
// the repair keeps exactly one claim over it —
// `test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness` — which asserts the shape the live host
// actually gets (every root-derived path under the lab root, the owner, the group, the declared modes) as a
// PURE DERIVATION: no process, no file, no `install`. That claim is what makes deleting the real derivation
// fail a control, and it is the reason supplying a root is not the same act as deleting one.

data a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host" as NonEmptyStr,

  // FOUND ON THE LADDER, AT MITIGATABLE — and the mitigation is what hides it. Each function is named by a
  // `gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` exclusion row and a `v2.workflow.local_repo_wet_terminal` schedule row: the
  // corpus's own statement that this claim runs, carried by a human reading a schedule and remembering that a
  // wet claim has a host. What the schedule points at is a target the claim can only reach on the live host, so
  // the mitigation is what makes the absence of execution invisible — a file in the schedule is what everyone
  // reads as "run". The ceiling is MechanicallyPreventable: the two questions in the header are decidable by
  // reading the witness's declared target against the production layout, with no judgment about what the witness
  // is for, which is the definition of a boundary that closes mechanically rather than by a reviewer
  // remembering to ask.
  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the capability to refuse a wet witness whose install target the production model also hands to a privileged writer: a corpus-side check that, for every registered wet claim, resolves each root the claim writes under to a value SUPPLIED BY THE CLAIM (an mktemp leaf under a scratch prefix, a `$TMPDIR` parent, or a host-said principal) rather than a projection of a production host layout, and refuses the row when any such root is reachable from a production provision parent — `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent` and its siblings in `gunbc.host.host_layout` — the same way the production layout in `gunbc.host.host_layout` names the roots an account does not own, together with the schedule-side check that any witness needing an ungranted-versus-granted pair names a non-live host in its `v2.workflow.local_repo_wet_terminal` row. SUFFICIENT FOR: the srv2 red becomes a refusal at the schedule, naming the path, instead of nine install failures that read like a bug; and the srv1 PASS becomes impossible to obtain silently. INDIFFERENT to: whether the witness takes its root from mktemp, from `$TMPDIR`, or from a declared scratch prefix, and which account it nominates, so long as neither arrives through a production layout projection. It retires no claim: the confinement legs keep asserting uid, gid, octal modes, the writable worktree and the refusal outside it — they only stop inheriting WHERE from production."
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    // THE RED, AND THE OBSERVATION THAT FILED THIS ROW. Nine install claims in
    // `test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness` on srv2, 2026-11-01, all
    // `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied`; the same target is carried by the
    // spawn claims in `test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness` through
    // `wet_lab_local_instance`, which copied `lab.provision_parent_root` and `lab.instance_root` while
    // overriding only `repo_root`. srv2 has user `briansrls`, no group `node`, no `gunbc-worker`, no
    // `gunbc-attempt`. Reported by jolly-hawk-60; operator relay stern-ram-763 approved the repair.
    "OBSERVED srv2 2026-11-01: 9 install claims of test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness fail `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied`; srv2 carries no group `node` and neither gunbc-worker nor gunbc-attempt, and srv1 — the only host where those claims could write — is live.",
    // THE REPAIR, AND ITS RECEIPT: the witnesses now SUPPLY their root and principal, and the live derivation
    // keeps exactly one execution, as a pure derivation.
    "REPAIRED BY PR #15627 (session zesty-stag-715): test.claim.roadmap.witness_supplied_instance supplies the instance root from an mktemp leaf under a declared scratch prefix and the principal from what the host SAYS, and builds the instance through the same production derivation the lab uses (isolated_dashboard_instance_admitting) so only the ROOTS and the PRINCIPAL come from the witness; test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness keeps the one pure-derivation claim over srv1_lab_dashboard_instance, writing nothing; test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness now takes both from the fixture.",
    // THE CONTROL, WHICH IS THE HALF OF THE REPAIR THAT MAKES IT A REPAIR. Pointing the supplied root back at
    // the live derivation, or dropping the supplied principal, makes the claims REFUSE — they do not fall back
    // to the production value.
    "CONTROL PR #15627: with the supplied root reverted to srv1_lab_dashboard_instance's own provision_parent_root/instance_root, and separately with the supplied principal dropped, the supplied-root witness refuses rather than running the claim at the production target; restoring the fixture returns the batch to green.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance", decl_name: "srv1_dashboard_lab_root"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance", decl_name: "srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_dashboard_instance", decl_name: "srv1_lab_dashboard_instance"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.roadmap.witness_supplied_instance", decl_name: "witness_supplied_instance"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness", decl_name: "dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_shapes"),
  ],
}
